There is a disturbing long-term (at least 30 years) trend in American
dictionaries to change preferred meanings and pronunciations if enough
people get them wrong for long enough.  Some argue that this is
"evolution" of the language, whereas others (I included) think it is
devolution.  Examples are:

February -- first pronunciation is now "feb - yoo - ary"
culinary -- now "cull - i - nary"

Latin-based words will fall sooner or later, when "even" scientists get
them wrong, along with such hard ones as "principle/principal".

My apologies that the above "datums" are unrelated to S, even if they are
about syntax.

