Appalled by my own weakness, I can not resist also making a brief
comment.  It seems there are some passionate grammarians on the list.
An American, I nevertheless share Don's view about the laxness of our
dictionaries, and always use data as a plural.  But there is another
common (mis)usage that stiffens my spine even more: the use of "email."
Being a contraction of "electronic mail," it should be used as "mail"
is used.  (Of course, we could look back at the evolution of the
irregular way in which the word "mail" came to be used).  Some examples:

  What you often hear: "Send me an email"
  What you should hear: "Send me email" or "Send me an email message"
  In analogy to: "Send me mail"

  What you often hear: "I've received lots of emails"
  What you should hear: "I've received lots of email" or
                        "I've received lots of email messages"
  In analogy to: "I've received lots of mail"

Ugh.  Apologies, and thanks for indulging me.  Having gotten that off my
chest, I can return to work...

Cheerio, Rick Rodgers

