I must object.  There are some subtleties being missed here. The data is/are
controversy is more than lax usage, poor education in grammar, and giving in to
popular speech patterns. The concept of data is now a more abstract concept 
than it
was 50, 40, even 30 years ago.  'Data' used to refer a set of individual pieces 
of
information, each of which we called a 'datum'.  Thus 'data' was just the 
plural of
'datum'.  But now 'data' also taken on a more abstract meaning of organized
information for a purpose, without any reference to any constituent 'datum'. In
this sense, the singular form is OK, and I would say preferable.  IMHO, there 
would
be no error in stating "Data is required to resolve this problem, but the 
current
data are too ambiguous to do so."

Enough pontificating.  Statistics are a wonderful discipline.
Joe

