Dear S+ users:  

Since everyone has put in their two-cents I feel obliged to follow suit. 
I would disagree with Joseph's definition that 'data' are defined as a set
of individual pieces of 'information'.  Data are a set of individual
observations and/or measurements, analysis of which can provide
information about the observed/measured phenomenon. 

What you may find interesting is that "datum" is also a strict geodetic
term but is often used to refer to a plurality of observations: In
geography, we refer STRICTLY to a 'datum' as a single point with
known coordinates, to which an ellipsoid, approximating the shape of the
earth, is attached. For example, the North American Datum of 1927 has its
initial point at Meades Ranch in the state of Kansas.  From this known
point all other points can be determined horizontally on the ellipsoidal
surface using azimuth's and distances among other paramaters.  Thus, from
the 'datum point' horizontal control networks are constructed which are
used in surveying and mapping - e.g., the NAD27 reference frame/horizontal
control network.  However, in most Geographical Information Systems
software and in common dialog among geomatics users we refer to the NAD27
Datum (or others like the NAD83 datum).  Consequently, in this respect and
erronously so, a set of measured locations (or data) is referred to as a
singular 'datum'. 

regards,
