From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Tue Nov 14 11:06:19 2000
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian Ripley)
Date: Tue, 14 Nov 2000 11:06:19 +0000
Subject: RE: scan
Message-ID: <200011141106.LAA03723@toucan.stats.ox.ac.uk>

> From: aspano at consodata.it
> Date: Tue, 14 Nov 2000 11:46:19 -0800
> 
> I am testing S+6
> I can open the connection with file
> > inCon <- file("dfile", open=T)
> > outCon <- file("outfile", open=T)

You want to open inCon for reading, outCon for writing.

> 
> then I can start a loop
> > for (i in 1:n){
> mydf <- scan(inCon, what=mydf.what, widths=mydf.widths)
> #transform my data
> > myOut <- myfun(mydf)
> #and then write the otput line to the output connection
> > 
> }
> 
> I am not sure about what to loop on...
> How does S+ undersytand that it has to move to the next line of the file at
> each step of the loop?

It knows to read/write the file where it left off last time.  The file
is open, just like in C code.  That's the great advantage of
connections.

Here's a real example in 6.0

cat(file="dfile", rnorm(100), sep="\n")
inCon <- file("dfile", open="r")
outCon <- file("outfile", open="w")
for(i in 1:1e6) {
  mydf <- scan(inCon, what=0, n = 1, sep="\n")
  if(!length(mydf)) break
  myOut <- c(i, mydf^2)
  write(myOut, file=outCon)
}
close(inCon)
close(outCon)

It really isn't hard if you read Chambers (1998).  The S-PLUS 6.0
on-line documentation is incomplete, though.

