> with predict.glm() on the same data set it was developed from to check
> its accuracy, and get some pretty weird numbers (-0.15, 1.2, -3.3, 2.27,
> etc).  The proposed benefit of logistic regression is that it is not
> suppose to return values <0 or >1.  As a comparison, I created linear
> regression equations with lm() and got values between 0 and 1 with
> predict() that were pretty reasonable predictions.  Even though I've
> read and followed the Help, the S-Plus Manuals, MASS, and other
> references, I'm sure I'm doing something wrong.  Can anyone guess?

You predicted the link, not the response?  The default is to
predict logit(p) not p.  As ever, it helps to show what you actually did.

> I also tried to find a reference to the logistic regression equation
> used in S-PLUS.  In 2 unrelated references I found y ~ 1/[1+exp[-(B0 +
> B1X1 + B2X2 +...)]] and y ~ 1/[1+exp(B0 + B1X1 + B2X2 + ...)], where B0
> is the intercept, and the other Bs are the constants for the predictors
> X.  Which one does S-Plus use?

Depends what you think y is  The first (e^eta/(1+e^eta)) is standard,
and that's what used where y = 1 for success and 0 for failure.
Be careful if you give y as a factor or matrix to be sure what `success'
is.

> Also, if any S-Plus developers are listening, I could not use the GUI to
> create the logistic equations because it would not open the formula
> builder - same for linear regression.  Any clues?

No.  It works on my 2000r3 system.

> Thanks for any help on this.  If I can't get a reasonable logistic
> regression, I'm going back to linear, which really isn't appropriate for
> binary data and probability forecasts.

Look at the predictions in MASS3 p.219, for example.  Those details do
matter.

